# 3. Destructuring

**Destructuring** allows us to extract values from arrays or properties from objects and assign them to variables.

There are two main types:

```text
Object Destructuring
Array Destructuring
```

---

## 🔹 Object Destructuring

Consider this object:

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Abhinav",
    age: 20,
    skill: "JavaScript"
};
```

Without destructuring:

```javascript
const name = user.name;
const age = user.age;
const skill = user.skill;
```

With destructuring:

```javascript
const { name, age, skill } = user;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);
console.log(skill);
```

Output:

```text
Abhinav
20
JavaScript
```

---

## 🔹 Renaming Variables

We can give a different variable name while destructuring.

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Abhinav"
};

const { name: userName } = user;

console.log(userName);
```

Output:

```text
Abhinav
```

---

## 🔹 Array Destructuring

Consider:

```javascript
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];
```

We can extract values like this:

```javascript
const [first, second, third] = numbers;

console.log(first);
console.log(second);
console.log(third);
```

Output:

```text
10
20
30
```

---

## 🔹 Skipping Values

We can skip an array element using an empty position.

```javascript
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];

const [first, , third] = numbers;

console.log(first);
console.log(third);
```

Output:

```text
10
30
```

---

## 🔥 Destructuring in React

Destructuring is extremely common with React props.

```javascript
function UserCard({ name, age }) {
    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{name}</h2>
            <p>{age}</p>
        </div>
    );
}
```

Instead of:

```javascript
function UserCard(props) {
    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{props.name}</h2>
            <p>{props.age}</p>
        </div>
    );
}
```

